# Credentials
.env
.env-github/
/.claude

# An ALKYON_SOURCES import file, which does carry passwords. Anchored to the root
# on purpose: a project's `.alkyon/sources.json` holds no credential and is meant
# to be committed.
/sources.json
# The contents, not the directory: git cannot re-include a file whose parent is
# excluded, and the one file worth committing lives in here.
.alkyon/*
!.alkyon/sources.json

# Rust
/target

# Tauri writes its permission schemas here at build time. Generated from the
# config and the plugins in use, so it is an artefact of the build rather than
# something to review — alkyon declares no commands and uses no plugins, which is
# why the capabilities file it describes does not exist.
/gen

# Release artefacts. The Linux binary is built in a container whose target
# directory is a Docker volume, so the only way back to the host is through the
# bind mount of this repository — it lands here rather than under /target.
/dist

# The grid bundle's build dependencies. Its *output* is committed under
# src/ui/vendor/, so building or running alkyon never needs npm — only changing
# the grid does.
/tools/grid/node_modules

# Scratch notes on what is left to do
/idea

# Sample CSV/JSON/parquet/Excel to click around in, written by
# `python docker/seed/make-sample-files.py`. Regenerated on demand, so the script
# is the artefact worth committing and its output is not.
/sample-data

# Carries the GitHub App ids and reads the key beside them. Neither is a secret,
# but it belongs next to the credentials it uses rather than in the history.
.github-release.py

# Release artefacts, built into the working tree and uploaded from it. The
# installer needs its own line: it ends in `-setup.exe`, so the glob above --
# anchored on `-x86_64.exe` -- does not reach it.
alkyon-*-x86_64
alkyon-*-x86_64.exe
alkyon-*-setup.exe
SHA256SUMS
